title: 3. A plan is one note of a page or two, whose headlines are its decisions.
pid: 52f44494-0400-4a4b-9837-b791929bf77c
body: |2-
  It sits in its author's graph, under "what I know". In order:
  - its state, in the first line of its body: proposed, accepted,
    built, or abandoned;
  - the request, linked, and the appetite;
  - goals, and non-goals: what it could reasonably do and will not;
  - the decisions, each a headline that states it, its body giving
    the alternatives rejected and why, and whether it is a one-way door;
  - questions for Jeff, as few as can be, each with the answer the
    author recommends and what gets built if he does not answer;
  - the building missions, each with its objective, what it may
    touch, and its check: a test to pass, or what to run and see.
  Rests on: read (Google: alternatives are the most important part,
  non-goals, the 1-3 page mini design doc; Nygard's decision records;
  Oxide's states; Anthropic's research system: a brief needs an
  objective, an output, its tools and its boundaries, and vague briefs
  caused duplicated work; GitHub's spec-driven development: tasks
  small enough to check in isolation).
